    "title": "How to Protect Your Algarve Home While Living Abroad",
    "summary": "A practical system covering access, inspections, contractors, reporting and arrival planning.",
    "categories": [
      "Ownership",
      "Guides"
    ],
    "slug": "protect-algarve-home-living-abroad",
    "date": "2026-05-29",
    "author": "Hugo Gonçalves",
    "content": "Protecting a home abroad is less about one device and more about a dependable system. Alarms and sensors can be valuable, but they do not tighten a loose shutter, smell a drain or meet a plumber at the gate.\n\n## Keep an accurate property file\n\nDocument keys, alarm instructions, water and electricity controls, equipment, warranties and contractor contacts. Update the file after changes. New owners can start with a [property handover](/property-handover-algarve/) to establish a baseline.\n\n## Control access\n\nAvoid keys labelled with a complete address. Decide who may authorise entry, how access is recorded and what should be checked after a contractor leaves. Informal key circulation creates uncertainty when an urgent visit is needed.\n\n## Use scheduled human inspections\n\nA regular visual check complements technology. It can identify visible water, moisture, damage, mail accumulation and changes around the exterior. Reports should include photographs and priority rather than a message saying only that everything “looks fine”.\n\n## Keep specialist responsibilities clear\n\nMaintain direct agreements with appropriate pool, garden, alarm and technical providers. A property care contact can coordinate them without implying responsibility for specialist workmanship.\n\n## Plan travel days\n\nUse [arrival preparation](/arrival-preparation-algarve/) before flights and a departure check afterwards. That turns each absence into a managed period rather than a gap in knowledge.",
